From a0940ce6f7258c7934294fbac1279dfb176acc4d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 04:42:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [VideoPlayer][GBM] SDR video of BT.2020 primaries sent in
 BT.2020 as it is

Without HDR passed through, the GLES renderer draws every picture in BT.709
(LoadShaders()) and tells the display so (DrawnColorSpace()): an SDR video
of BT.2020 primaries, as on a few Ultra HD Blu-rays and some streams, loses
its colours outside BT.709, which are clipped.

With the new setting "Keep the wide colours of SDR videos (BT.2020)"
(videoscreen.hadessdrbt2020: System > Display, advanced, off by default),
such a video goes out as it is where the display can take it: the renderer
draws it in its own primaries (DrawnPrimaries()), the display is told
BT.2020 (BT2020_RGB in the Colorspace property of the connector), and the
GUI and the subtitles are converted over it by the SDR mode of the GUI
composite. Only where the EDID declares BT.2020 RGB and the connector
offers BT2020_RGB; not while native Dolby Vision output or its menus hold
the output, nor on a 3D output; and only for a picture that is Kodi's
ordinary output (not Dolby Vision rebuilt, nor HDR mapped to SDR by the
bridge). Anything else, and a composite that cannot be set up, leaves the
video drawn in BT.709 as before. Off by default because a display that
declares BT.2020 may still show SDR in it badly: it is to be seen on one.

kodi.log says once a video which way it goes and why. During the video it
goes back to BT.709, with a line, where the picture stops being SDR of
BT.2020, where the composite is not on any more, or where the output goes
into 3D. advancedsettings.xml <video><sdrbt2020test> leaves out the
questions to the display and the driver, for a test on a virtual GPU.

The decision and the arithmetic are in xbmc/utils/SdrWide.h, tested on the
host with the matrix derived from the chromaticities. With the setting off
nothing changes. The idea of a BT.2020 destination is CroqueMr's (#153 of
his build); dtop129 reported the colours of such videos there (#210).
